    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Passive avoidance is a learned response to an aversive stimulus in which the animal has learned to avoid the stimulus by not approaching or engaging with it. Here, we develop a modified passive avoidance protocol for hamsters to assess long term passive avoidance performance over the course of 28 days. Shocked hamsters retained memory of an aversive foot shock over a long-term period of time. Since most memory is subject to circadian modulation, we investigated whether passive avoidance performance of hamsters varied between the morning (AM) and afternoon (PM). We demonstrate that there is no time of day effect to passive avoidance performance, and this suggests that there is no circadian modulation of passive avoidance in hamsters. We also demonstrate that there is no variation in passive avoidance performance between sexes. Thus, this modified passive avoidance test can be used in the future to assess the long-term memory performance of Siberian hamsters.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
